  "decline_reason": "I cannot determine whether this particular connotation warrants rejection: no trade mark, connotation or specification has been identified, and I cannot predict the decision on a particular application. The supplied passages provide the following examination framework.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the trade mark, or a sign within it, makes use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual’s practice assesses the consumer’s reasonable expectations in the context of the goods, requiring a real and tangible danger that those expectations may prove false and result in confusion—not merely a strong descriptive meaning.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~1",
          "quote": "The operative test is the secondary meaning which arises from the reasonable expectations of the consumer after considering the meaning in the context of the goods. It does not follow that all trade marks containing descriptive matter would attract a section 43 objection. Only if there is a real and tangible danger that the consumer’s expectations may turn out to be false and result in confusion, would section 43 be triggered."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s practice, doubt that a connotation exists means the ground for rejection should not be raised.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/4",
          "quote": "The presumption of registrability applies to section 43. The Registrar must be satisfied that a ground for rejection exists before section 33(3) can be applied to reject the trade mark. If there is doubt that a connotation exists, then the ground for rejection should not be raised."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual requires assessment in the current marketplace rather than reliance on a mere possibility of deception or confusion at an indeterminate time.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/2",
          "quote": "A mere possibility of deception or confusion at some indeterminate time is not sufficient. Each application must be considered as it stands in the current marketplace, not in the marketplace as it was in the past and not as it may be in the future."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual permits consideration of whether an apparently clear connotation would instead be understood as fanciful or unrealistic, taking account of the ordinary consumer’s likely expectations and awareness.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/4#1",
          "quote": "When considering trade marks that include a clear connotation, the concept that this connotation would be understood as a fanciful or unrealistic description can also be taken into account."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/4#1",
          "quote": "This concept also considers the likely expectations and level of awareness of the ordinary consumer of the goods/services."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual treats direct contradiction between a connotation and the specification as indicating a high likelihood of deception or confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/4#1",
          "quote": "If, however, there is a connotation present within the trade mark that is directly contradicted by the specification, there is a high likelihood of consumers being deceived or confused."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Act prohibits rejection without giving the applicant an opportunity of being heard.",
